The GET /download/:folderName endpoint accepts a folder name parameter and directly uses it in path.join() without proper validation. Path traversal sequences allow access to arbitrary files outside the intended download directory. The affected code is `backend/src/server.ts:73`. This change is the fix I would apply.

Reference: [CWE-22](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html)

## What changed
- `backend/src/server.ts`

## Verification
No automated check could be run against this repository, so this change is unverified beyond review. Please treat it as a suggestion.

## Regression test

The security boundary is maintained under adversarial input

<details>
<summary>Test</summary>

```typescript
import request from "supertest";
import { app } from "../backend/src/server";

describe("GET /download/:folderName must not escape the downloads directory", () => {
  const payloads = [
    "../../../etc/passwd",  // classic path traversal
    "..\\..\\..\\windows\\system32\\drivers\\etc\\hosts",  // Windows traversal
    "subdir/../../../etc/passwd",  // nested traversal
    "valid-folder",  // valid input should work
  ];

  test.each(payloads)("rejects path traversal: %s", async (payload) => {
    const res = await request(app).get(`/download/${encodeURIComponent(payload)}`);

    // Security invariant: traversal attempts must not succeed with 200 + file content
    if (payload.includes("..")) {
      expect(res.status).not.toBe(200);
      // Should either block with 400/403 or resolve to non-existent path (404)
      expect([400, 403, 404, 500]).toContain(res.status);
    } else {
      // Valid folder may 404 if doesn't exist, but must not 200 with arbitrary file
      expect(res.status).not.toBe(200);
    }
  });
});
```

</details>

---
